  "ch2.say.o0": "At <code>-O0</code>, {allocas} local slots occupy {bytes} stack bytes: running {it} iterations executes <em>{stack} redundant stack loads/stores</em> plus {arr} array loads ({total} total memory ops).",
  "ch2.say.o2": "After register promotion, <code>alloca</code> drops to zero: all {stack} local stack accesses vanish, leaving only the {arr} true array loads—cutting <em>{saved} memory ops</em> compared to -O0's {o0}.",
  "ch2.p3": "Over just 8 iterations in our default loop, <code>-O0</code> executes 79 IR memory ops while real work needs only 8 array loads—wasting 71 stack round-trips purely shuffling local variables.",
  "ch2.limit": "The model omits hardware L1 data caches and store-to-load forwarding buffers (which hide part of <code>-O0</code>'s latency on real silicon) as well as unrolled vector expansion at <code>-O2</code>.",
  "ch3.title": "Static Single Assignment (SSA) & `mem2reg`: Where `phi` Nodes Live",
  "toc.ch3": "SSA form and mem2reg",
  "ch3.law": "Static Single Assignment requires every virtual register to be defined exactly once; when control paths assign different versions and rejoin, <code>mem2reg</code> inserts a <code>phi</code> instruction at the dominance frontier to select the incoming value.",
  "ch3.p1": "Promoting straight-line <code>alloca</code> code is easy: <code>x = 1; x = x + 2;</code> simply renames to <code>%x.0 = 1; %x.1 = add %x.0, 2</code>. The catch appears at an <code>if-else</code> diamond: if the left branch sets <code>x = b * 2</code> and the right branch sets <code>x = a - 3</code>, which virtual register should <code>return x + 5</code> read after the paths merge?",
  "ch3.p2": "LLVM solves this with two tightly coupled rules. First, <strong>Static Single Assignment (SSA)</strong>: every virtual register (<code>%x.0</code>, <code>%x.1</code>, ...) has <strong>at most one definition</strong> in the function. Finding where an operand came from becomes an immediate pointer jump rather than scanning backward through basic blocks to make sure nothing overwrote it. Second, the <strong><code>phi</code> node</strong>: placed at the top of a merge block, <code>%x.3 = phi i32 [ %x.1, %B1 ], [ %x.2, %B2 ]</code> picks <code>%x.1</code> if control arrived from <code>%B1</code>, or <code>%x.2</code> if control arrived from <code>%B2</code>.",
  "dg.ssa.cap": "Figure 3. Diamond Control-Flow Graph (CFG) and dominance frontier. Block B0 dominates B1, B2, and B3, whereas neither B1 nor B2 dominates B3—making B3 their dominance frontier where <code>mem2reg</code> inserts a <code>phi</code> node.",
  "dg.ssa.aria": "Diagram: CFG diamond from entry B0 through B1 and B2 to merge block B3 marked as dominance frontier with a phi node",
  "dg.ssa.df": "Dominance frontier (insert phi)",
  "ch3.p3": "<strong>How does the compiler know exactly which blocks need a `phi`?</strong> Using <strong>Dominance Frontiers</strong> (Cytron et al., 1991). Block <code>X</code> dominates block <code>Y</code> if every path from the entry block to <code>Y</code> must pass through <code>X</code>. Block <code>Y</code> lies in <code>X</code>'s dominance frontier if <code>X</code> dominates a predecessor of <code>Y</code> without strictly dominating <code>Y</code> itself (for instance, <code>then</code> feeds <code>merge</code>, yet <code>entry</code> can bypass <code>then</code> via <code>else</code>). That boundary is precisely where a definition inside <code>X</code> collides with an alternate path, requiring a <code>phi</code>. In host Apple Clang 21.0.0's optimization pipeline, <code>SROAPass</code> handles aggregate splitting and promotes non-escaped stack slots directly into SSA form.",
  "ch3.model.title": "Dominance Frontier Calculator & SSA phi Inserter",
  "ch3.model.how": "Switch between four CFG topologies (diamond, if-then, loop back-edge, and chained branches) and toggle mem2reg to watch the real dominance-frontier and stack-renaming algorithm place [DF] phi nodes and erase stack accesses.",
