  "ch4.detail.instcombine_cse": "InstCombine turns adding a value to itself into shl 1, reaching the fixed point.",
  "ch4.say.step": "After <em>{pass}</em>: {ops} IR instructions remain ({removed} removed from initial {start}). {detail}",
  "ch4.say.fixed": "Fixed point reached at <em>{pass}</em>: the function shrank from {start} instructions down to <em>{ops}</em>. {detail}",
  "ch4.p5": "This \"many small passes in cycles\" architecture explains why compile time jumps between `-O2` and `-O3`: passes rely on simple local pattern matchers (LLVM's `InstCombine` alone spans tens of thousands of lines of rules) and need repeated sweeps so earlier simplifications trigger later ones.",
  "ch4.limit": "The model omits LLVM's CGSCC pass-manager invalidation (`PreservedAnalyses`), heuristic inline cost budgets, and `InstCombine`'s internal worklist iteration.",
  "ch5.title": "Loop Optimizations: LICM, Pointer Aliasing, and SIMD Vectorization",
  "toc.ch5": "Loop hoisting, aliasing & SIMD",
  "ch5.law": "Loops dominate runtime, yet whether LLVM can hoist invariant work out of a loop (LICM) or pack iterations into 4-wide SIMD vector lanes depends first on proving that pointers cannot alias in memory.",
  "ch5.p1": "Look at this three-line loop (nearly identical to our live `hoist_demo` vs `hoist_restrict` probe):",
  "ch5.p2": "<pre class=\"code\">for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) {   out[i] = a[i] + (<em>b </em> 2); }</pre>",
  "ch5.p3": "Intuitively, `<em>b </em> 2` stays constant across all `n` iterations: LLVM should compute it once in the loop <strong>Preheader</strong>, keep it in a register, and vectorize the loop into `&lt;4 x i32&gt;` ARM64 NEON additions (as covered in <a href=\"../../systems/how-a-cpu-does-math/#ch9\">SIMD instructions</a> and <a href=\"../../systems/how-a-cpu-does-math/#ch10\">when compilers auto-vectorize</a>). Yet inspecting Apple Clang 21.0.0's `-O2` IR on `hoist_demo` reveals a critical catch. Because `a`, `b`, and `out` all point to `int` without `restrict`, Clang cannot statically prove that writing `out[0]` does not overwrite `<em>b`! If `</em>b` changed on step 0, hoisting it outside would miscompile step 1. As a result, Clang emits a <strong>runtime pointer-overlap check</strong> (`icmp ult ptr`) before the loop: if ranges do not overlap, execution enters a fast vectorized path; if they do overlap, it falls back to a scalar loop reloading `*b` on every iteration. Adding `restrict` (`ptr noalias` in IR) removes the runtime guard completely.",
  "dg.licm.cap": "Figure 5. Three-part optimized loop layout: LICM hoists invariant <code><em>b </em> 2</code> into the preheader, LoopVectorize widens the body into 128-bit <code>&lt;4 x i32&gt;</code> lanes, and a scalar tail handles leftovers.",
  "dg.licm.aria": "Diagram: preheader block computing invariant once, vector loop processing four integers per iteration, scalar tail for remainder",
  "dg.licm.pre": "Preheader (runs once)",
  "dg.licm.vec": "Vector loop (4× per step)",
  "dg.licm.tail": "Scalar epilogue tail",
  "dg.licm.taild": "Handles remaining N mod 4 items",
  "ch5.p4": "<strong>Type-Based Alias Analysis (TBAA)</strong> provides a second escape hatch: under C/C++ strict aliasing rules, pointers to incompatible types (such as `float<em>` and `int</em>`) are assumed not to alias, unlocking unconditional hoisting without `restrict`. On `n = 20` elements with 4-lane NEON, 100 unoptimized loop ops drop to 5 vector batches (17 total ops).",
  "ch5.model.title": "LICM Hoisting, Alias Guards & SIMD Lane Packing",
  "ch5.model.how": "Drag array size N, pick vector width (1 / 4 / 8 lanes), and switch pointer aliasing assumptions (may-alias vs restrict vs TBAA) to inspect runtime overlap guards, invariant *b reloads, and vector speedup.",
